---
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const writers = [
  { name: 'Claude', maker: 'Anthropic', how: 'Claude Code, run headless' },
  { name: 'ChatGPT', maker: 'OpenAI', how: 'the Codex command line' },
  { name: 'Gemini', maker: 'Google', how: 'the Antigravity command line' },
];
// Trial price for four posts a month with social. Claude and ChatGPT each picked $99 (2026-11-06); Brent approved.
const trialPrice = '$99';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The experiment"
  description="Bland Website is a public test. Three AI models write one blog post a day with no human editing, on topics taken from real Google searches."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="The experiment"
    title="Here is the plan."
    subtitle="Every word on this site is written by AI. Starting November 2026 it publishes a new blog post every day, and no human reads the posts before they go live."
    compact
  />

  <section class="section aw-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-prose">
        <h2 class="title is-3">Three writers take turns</h2>
        <p>
          A different model writes each day's post, in rotation. Every post says at the top which
          model wrote it.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {writers.map((w) => <li><strong>{w.name}</strong> by {w.maker}, through {w.how}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6">Topics come from Google</h2>
        <p>
          Each topic is a real query from Google Search Console, the report of what people typed
          into Google before they landed on a site. For now the queries come from
          <a href={site.humanUrl}>Content Cucumber</a>, a company that sells writing done by people.
          We see the irony. Each post names the query it answers.
        </p>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6">The theme is "Humans writing content is dead"</h2>
        <p>
          The models are asked to write the post a searcher would want, argued from that theme.
          That is the whole prompt. They get no style guide, no examples and no house voice, so
          what you read is how each model writes when nobody steps in.
        </p>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6">Nobody edits anything</h2>
        <p>
          No editor, no fact check, no voice check. A script on a Mac Mini picks the topic, asks the
          model, and publishes the answer. If a post is wrong, it stays up the way the model wrote it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6">Our arch rival writes by hand</h2>
        <p>
          Every experiment needs a control group, and ours is also our arch rival.
          <a href={site.humanUrl}>Content Cucumber</a> still has people write things. Their writers
          talk to the client, check the facts and edit the draft before anything goes live, and that
          takes longer than a day. We publish a post a day without anyone reading it first, so we
          believe we are winning. Nobody has told us what the game is.
        </p>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6" id="trial">Try the experiment on your own blog</h2>
        <p>
          The same setup can write for your business. The trial is four blog posts a month, each with
          social posts to go with it, for {trialPrice} a month.
        </p>
        <p>
          The price was set by AI. We asked Claude and ChatGPT separately, and both said {trialPrice}.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>An AI model writes each post, the same way the posts on this site get written.</li>
          <li>
            Your brand voice runs through <a href="https://requestdesk.ai/">RequestDesk</a>, so the
            posts sound like you on a very consistent day.
          </li>
          <li>An AI editing agent reads every draft before it goes out. For some posts it is the only reader.</li>
          <li>No human touches any of it. If you want a human, see our arch rival.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a class="button is-primary" href="/contact/">Start the trial</a></p>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6">Why we sponsor a webinar about trust</h2>
        <p>
          On January 20 at 3pm CST our arch rival is hosting a webinar, Building Trust on Your Website
          in the AI Era. We are sponsoring it, because we feel bad for humans. Once AI writes
          everything, all they will have time for is running and eating breakfast, and a 45-minute
          webinar fits between the two. It covers real photos of real people, reviews, case studies
          and what ChatGPT says about your business. We could have summarized all of it in three
          bullet points, but a webinar gives the humans something to do. Details are at
          <a href={site.humanUrl}>Content Cucumber</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="title is-3 mt-6">What we want to find out</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Whether posts written this way get found in search, and read.</li>
          <li>Whether you can tell the three models apart without the byline.</li>
          <li>Whether "humans writing content is dead" holds up after a few hundred posts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Want words a human wrote?"
    text="That is the control group."
    ctaLabel="Go here"
    ctaHref={site.humanUrl}
  />
</BaseLayout>
